    "turn_text": "So this week we're going to do something a little different. We're going to have a conversation with former Republican Congressman Bob Inglis from South Carolina who has become a huge climate change advocate and is involved on the front lines of the climate struggle in terms of policy solutions involving the private sector, he's a former Republican congressman from South Carolina, served from, he got elected in 92, lost a U.S. Senate race to Fritz Hollings, he was the Republican nominee in 98th, lost to Fritz Hollings, he was the longtime Democratic Senator in South Carolina, and that was Ernest Hollings final term, and then he came back, when Jim DeMintran for the Senate took his old seat, Greenville Spartanburg area, seat back in 2004. And it was much more moderate if you want to look at ideology in his second stanza in the second, second, six-year period in the house and got beaten a primary by Trey Gowdy in 2010, the Tea Party year. You know, in terms of ideology, I would call him a traditional conservative, but he's a guy that I think on the current ideological scale is very middle of the road in terms of his ideology and policy preferences and in fact endorsed President Biden's 2020 campaign against Donald Trump. So anyway, very, very insightful, interesting conversation with him. We're about to have. So stay tuned. Congressman, you're involved now in a really exciting venture that you're the executive director of, which is so important when we talk about climate change and providing solutions and also educating conservatives, perhaps, about the issue and how we can develop some free market solutions to climate change.",
