    "turn_text": "his response to this challenge. And so that's how this guy that used to dispute climate change became a climate change, you know, when I was an activist, but basically a guy who says, we gotta do something about it. So that was my metamorphosis. And then you look at the politics of it. When I was running again in 04, it was okay that that change had happened. The economy was pretty good. People were pretty content with circumstances. And there's widespread agreement that we need to do something about climate change. After all, it was George H.W. Bush who had sort of first identified it at the White House level as something we need to do, act on climate change. And so then get on over there to like 2008. and to show how much consensus there was, like you were just referencing. Early 08, Newt Gingrich, former Speaker of the House, is on the couch with Nancy Pelosi, another, at that point, the Speaker of the House, saying, don't agree on much, do we, Newt? No, Nancy, but we agree climate change is real, and we need to do something about it. That was early 08. So there's broad consensus, just like you were just saying, that Republicans and Democrats are agreeing to do something. End of a wait, dude, it switched. We don't know, we said. And of course, the things that happened that it caused them to switch, I think, were the election of Barack Obama and the coming of the global financial crisis and the beginning of the Great Recession. And so, That's where we took this big, long detour into climate disputation. You know, we don't call it denial at the Republican.org because people who dispute climate change say that equates them with Holocaust denial. So we give them the courtesy of calling them disputters. They dispute the signs. And so we went through this time of them saying we don't. Basically, they were in aggressive disbelief about climate change. It was essentially, I don't believe in climate change, you shouldn't either. And it was because of the great recession we thought. Then, you know, things have changed and improved. It's now different. It's getting better. It's not quite there yet, but we're getting better because the Republicans now are more inclined Not to dispute the obvious about climate change. You know, when the water is 97, 98 degrees in some places, 100 degrees around South Florida, ocean water, it's hard to dispute the sounds going on. And so now the disputation is more like, yeah, but how we get China? And that's where we are now. We've gotten through that period of out and out denial of climate change because it's getting pretty obvious. And we're now in a situation of, yeah, devise a solution though that gets the world in. That's the challenge of the moment.",
