  {
    "episode_id": 83335,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83335.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, really, there are three ways to fix climate change. I know that sounds like an overstatement of how it would just be three. We have broadly speaking, there are three ways to fix it. One is you can attempt to regulate emissions. The problem is you can't regulate Chinese emissions from here in America. So that's sort of a hard come up and so that first option, right? Second, policy option is to incentivize clean energy. We have done this now in the inflation reduction act. And those are going to work for American tax paying firms because if you pay taxes in America, then tax credits in the American tax code are really powerful. But if you don't pay American taxes, say you're a Chinese company, those tax incentives don't help you and they don't change your economics. So we may, in that second option of, you know, incentive scope for clean energy, we may clean up American air, which would be a good thing. Fewer asthma cases, longer life expectancy, fewer hospitalizations, it would be good here in America to do those things. But we won't be solving climate change because devilish problem on climate change, you got to get the whole world in. So then the third approach, which obviously I favor now having just number one and number two, is to just put on a carbon tax, pair it with a reduction in payroll taxes, so there's no growth in government. And so according to the current pressure budget office, the bottom 70% of Americans would do better in that tax swap where you give them a fuller paycheck because you're reducing their payroll taxes or fight taxes. You know, on your check from your employer, it's 6.2% in the fight a box that you pay, but you're not seeing these employers paying the other 6.2%. So it's a whopping 12.4% tax. So our thought would be reduce that tax, put a tax on carbon dioxide instead, and then apply that tax to imports. And that can be done. And if it's done, then you get the whole world in, because that's the exciting thing about this third option is If you have that domestic carbon price, carbon tax, and since we're conservative as we want to pair it with an equal reduction in existing taxes, so there's no growth in government. But if you do that, then under World Trade Organization rules, it's okay to apply that tax imports coming from countries that don't collect the same carbon tax as we would have in their home countries. It makes it in the interest of our trading partners at that point to do the same thing that we're doing. And then the whole world is following. So it's a concrete example. There's a sheet of flat steel coming from China right now. Let's say it's coming through the port of Seattle. We apply our carbon tax, China objects in the world trade organization. They say, no, you can't do that. It's an impermissible tariff. We think they lose that case based on precedence in the chemical industry that say, oh no, you can have a content tax on the base and the content of the stuff coming in. And this would be a carbon content tax. If we're right and if it's upheld, join at 24 hours later. If you do reach consensus very efficiently in China, not a",
