  {
    "episode_id": 83335,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83335.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, you know, I think the most exciting thing happening right now is actually in the Senate where There's some Republicans, Kramer of North Dakota and Cassidy of Louisiana in particular, who are working on the, what I just referenced a minute ago, which is this carbon border adjustment mechanism. It sounds like a lot of words, but basically what it means is applying at our borders a corrective to Chinese imports or to imports from countries that don't have their own carbon tax. And so in the case of Kevin Kramer of North Dakota and Senator Cassidy Louisiana, you have them talking about a way of applying that tax. And I think that's really exciting. The first step of that is something called the Prove Act. Prove Act. And so the idea is to create good data here in the United States, gather good data. It sounds sort of won't keep it. It makes sense. As we need to know how many emissions we've got in American steel, for example, since I'm sticking with the steel as an example. And once that gather, that data is gathered, then we can know how we compare to other countries. which will be very important, especially as the European Union starts applying its own carbon tax on entry of our goods to the European Union. You know, starting this year, people that export stuff into the European Union have to substantiate their carbon footprint. They have to say, you know, in this year, if that's deal, this is how much carbon was emitted. Starting in 2026, If that steel is dirtier than European steel, then you'll have to pay essentially a carbon tax. If it's cleaner than European steel, then you won't have to pay the tax. So it's an approved act. It's a step toward helping us be ready for that action in Europe, which is a major trading partner for us. And also, I think in the process, what it's going to do is help us to see, hey, you know what? We could do what the Europeans are doing. We could do it to China. We can make it so that China and the rest of the world gets in on climate action. And again, that overcomes the major hurdle right now that we find of our fellow conservatives. You know, they've stopped arguing with thermometers pretty much and with yardsticks and rulers about sea level rise. But they do want to know how you get the world in. And we think that that's the work that Kevin Kramer doing in Senator Castro, you're doing, I think will help. Check her out, how to get the world in.",
